  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MMAAK HIV Prevention and Home Based Care Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project team continued to provide prevention, care and support services in the three divisions of Nyangoma, Usigu and Maranda in Bondo district. These were achieved through media campaigns, dialogue forums, awareness campaigns, lobbying and advocacy activities training, distribution of IEC materials and home based care. Activities targeted men and boys on their vulnerability to HIV infection, and PLWHA receiving ARVs to adopt safe practices in order to prevent re- infection while adhering to treatment. The activities ensured quality and timely service provision. The services not only improved the quality of life of PLWHA but also reduced care burden on women in infected and affected families. 

Male community volunteers were trained on HBC, Counselling, advocacy and support group management.  More men are motivated to work as volunteers, change agents and role models. Majority are HIV positive themselves. Service provision has witnessed involvement of men leading to increased number of men accessing HIV related services. Women have been brought on board to ensure complimentarily in service. Continuous skills building with regular monitoring and evaluation to ensure effective application and better service delivery is necessary. 

During this period, the project continued to scale up initiatives to reach out to a larger male population.  Linkages with churches and other institutions such as the constituency AIDS committees and schools have improved. Men have been mobilized and motivated as agents of change. Men have taken on active roles in HIV mitigation. Successes during this period include the change of attitude by the community towards PLWHA, leading to increased number of people seeking support services from the project. Stigma is reducing as demonstrated by greater involvement of PLWHA in care and support activities, open to discuss HIV and AIDS issues and their HIV status; and joining of support groups or group therapies.</narrative>
  </description>
